The '''government bonds of Balboa''' comprise the {{w|sovereign debt|sovereign debt securities}} issued by the [[Government of Balboa]] through the [[Ministry of Finance (Balboa)|Ministry of Finance]], as well as sub-sovereign bonds issued by provincial, comarca, and municipal authorities. As of 2026, total national government debt stands at approximately $119 billion (28% of nominal GDP), of which approximately $85.8 billion is in the form of negotiable bonds and treasury bills, with the remainder consisting of multilateral loans and crisis-era obligations. The Balboan government bond market is the deepest and most liquid in the [[BTPMEU]] region, and Balboa sovereign bonds serve as the benchmark for [[Abyala]]n fixed income pricing.

== Management and issuance ==

The issuance of government bonds is managed through a split arrangement between the [[Ministry of Finance (Balboa)|Ministry of Finance]] and the [[Bank of Balboa]], the country's central bank. The Ministry of Finance, through its Treasury division (Volisanian: ''Tresoreria''), determines debt management policy, including the annual borrowing programme, maturity mix, and instrument selection, in coordination with the national budget process. The Bank of Balboa, as fiscal agent, executes bond auctions, manages settlement and clearing, and operates the central securities depository for government debt.

The Treasury division was formally established as a distinct office within the Ministry of Finance in 1993, as part of the fiscal reforms that also introduced the national {{w|land value tax}}. Prior to its establishment, debt issuance was handled on an ad hoc basis by the Ministry's budget department. The creation of a dedicated treasury function was recommended by the [[Adonian Community Monetary Fund]] (ACMF) as part of its post-1994 reform programme, which sought to strengthen Balboa's fiscal institutions following the [[Balboa real-estate asset crisis|real-estate crisis]] and the [[1994 Balboa spot recession|1994 spot recession]].

The annual borrowing programme is set in conjunction with the budget. The Ministry of Finance publishes a quarterly issuance calendar indicating the instruments, amounts, and auction dates for the coming quarter, providing predictability for market participants. The Bank of Balboa conducts auctions on behalf of the Ministry, using a competitive bidding process in which primary dealers and institutional investors submit price and yield bids.

== Instruments ==

The Balboan government issues a range of debt instruments across the maturity spectrum, from short-term treasury bills to long-term bonds and inflation-linked securities.

=== Treasury bills ===

''Lletres del Tresor'' (Treasury bills) are short-term {{w|zero-coupon bond|discount securities}} with maturities of less than one year. Treasury bills are sold at a discount to face value and redeemed at par on maturity. Standard maturities are 3-month, 6-month, and 12-month. Treasury bills are used for cash flow management and short-term financing needs. They are the most liquid segment of the government debt market and serve as the benchmark for short-term interest rates in Balboa.

=== Treasury notes ===

''Bons del Tresor'' (Treasury notes) are medium-term fixed-coupon bonds with maturities ranging from 2 to 10 years. Treasury notes pay semi-annual coupons and are issued at or near par. Standard maturities are 2-year, 3-year, 5-year, 7-year, and 10-year. The 10-year treasury note is the benchmark instrument for long-term interest rate pricing in the Balboan market and is the most closely watched government bond yield.

=== Treasury bonds ===

''Obligacions del Tresor'' (Treasury bonds) are long-term fixed-coupon bonds with maturities exceeding 10 years. Standard maturities are 15-year, 20-year, and 30-year. Treasury bonds are issued less frequently than notes and are primarily held by long-term institutional investors such as the [[Balboa Pension Fund]] and insurance companies. The 30-year bond is the longest-dated government security in the Balboan market.

=== Inflation-linked bonds ===

''Bons del Tresor indexats'' (inflation-linked bonds) are {{w|inflation-indexed bond|inflation-indexed securities}} whose principal is adjusted in line with a designated inflation index. These bonds provide investors with protection against inflation risk and are typically issued in 5-year and 10-year maturities. The inflation-linked programme was introduced in the early 2000s as part of the government's strategy to diversify its funding sources and provide a wider range of instruments to investors.

=== Infrastructure bonds ===

''Bons d'infraestructura'' (infrastructure bonds) are project-specific bonds issued to finance designated infrastructure projects, including toll roads, port facilities, and rail infrastructure. Infrastructure bonds are backed by the revenue streams of the projects they finance, such as tolls and user fees, rather than by the general fiscal capacity of the government. Approximately $6.0 billion in infrastructure bonds is currently outstanding. These bonds typically carry maturities of 10 to 20 years and are held by a mix of institutional investors and project finance lenders.

